comments.body:
As I former Foreign Service Officer with boots on the ground in the Middle East (lunch and afternoon with David Ben-Gurion and book on Nasser’s Egypt, I have long been aware of the three-dimensional aspects of Middle East relationship .

There is nothing new in bizarre relationships and attempted assassinations. Indeed, in the late 50s there was a brief merger between Egypt and Yemen.

Mohamed bin Salman, who murdered a Washington Post journalist, was highlighted by #45 while MBS showered $$$ on Jared Kushner, Trump’s realtor son in law.

As #47 Trump treated MBS as his buddy. After Trump’s disastrous Iranian military ‘excursion,’ MBS was badly hit by the repercussions:
1) the US security umbrella was badly leaking and probably gone;
2) Saudi Arabia and other Arab oil countries so far have probably lost at least $400,000,000,000 in revenues and face continued revenue uncertainty;
3) MBS’s grandiose plans for a glittering new city, LIV, and other costly non-oil expenditures and been severely reduced;
4) the Houthi resurgence has directly affected SA’s security, including its energy facilities and major airports;
5) The prestigious World Petroleum Conference, scheduled for Riyadh, is now impacted by the attacks on the airport.

Trump’s fixation on finding a ‘win’ in a self-inflicted losing situation conflicts with MBS’s ambitions in the Middle East and globally.
